<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Icon colour for a bottom-navigation tab, by state.

    A *colour state list* rather than a plain colour: one resource that resolves to a different
    value depending on what the view is doing. The bottom bar needs exactly that — the selected tab
    must look different from the other three — and a selector keeps that rule in one file instead of
    having the Activity set colours by hand on every tab change.

    Order matters. Android walks the list top to bottom and takes the **first** item whose
    conditions all match, so the selected state has to come first: with the catch-all default above
    it, every tab would resolve to the unselected colour and nothing would ever look active.

    **Why fixed colours rather than theme attributes.** This bar is a dark capsule in light mode as
    well as dark — see the bottom-navigation note in colors.xml — so the roles that would normally be
    right here are not: ?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant is a dark grey in light mode and would leave
    three of the four tabs invisible against the bar.

    There used to be a second selector beside this one for the labels, because the two did not share a
    selected colour: the icon sat on the yellow pill and the label on the dark bar beside it. The bar
    no longer draws labels at all — see PetCare.BottomNav — so this is the only tint it needs.
-->
<selector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- The active tab's icon, drawn on the accent pill (see PetCare.BottomNav.ActiveIndicator), so
         it must use the colour guaranteed legible on the accent and nothing else. -->
    <item
        android:color="@color/nav_item_selected_icon"
        android:state_checked="true" />

    <!-- Everything else: quiet, so the active tab is the one the eye finds. -->
    <item android:color="@color/nav_item_unselected" />

</selector>
